er get to 1 by doubling from 2. Unless \"stop at singleton granularity\" means we stop when the list becomes a single element? Or when granularity = len(items)? Not clear.\n\nGiven the ambiguity, I need to deduce from the problem context. This seems like a typical \"chunk reduction\" task used in some fuzzing or minimization algorithms. Perhaps it's analogous to \"reduce the list by removing chunks while preserving some property\". The description: \"Apply deterministic chunk-removal and granularity-refinement passes while preserving a supplied syntax and interestingness oracle.\" The oracle is represented by the required_failure_items: they must remain. So our goal is to remove as many items as possible (but not necessarily all) while ensuring all required_failure_items are still present. The algorithm described is similar to \"binary search\" or \"delta debugging\" minimizer. In typical DD, you start with granularity = n/2, try each chunk, if a removal works you reduce the set and continue with same granularity; if not, you halve the granularity. Here they multiply by 2? Actually they double when no removal succeeds, which is opposite of typical DD. But they also say \"start at granularity two\" and \"stop at singleton granularity\" (size 1). That suggests the granularities are powers of 2? Starting at 2, then 4, then 8, then 16 etc. But stop at singleton (1) is ambiguous. Maybe they meant \"stop when granularity becomes 1\" meaning after doubling, if we ever get to 1 (which would happen if we start at 1? But start at 2). Another interpretation: \"granularity\" might refer to the number of chunks? For example, granularity = 2 means the list is divided into 2 chunks (like halves). Then \"slightly coarser\" means increase number of chunks? That would be finer. No.\n\nLet's look at the fixture payloads. They have items list and required_failure_items. The output should have reduced_items (a list of strings), attempts (integer count), one_minimal (boolean). The one_mi