r chunks first, then if possible, removes even larger chunks, but if not, splits into smaller chunks. In typical delta debugging, you start with one chunk (the whole set) and if that fails, you split into 2, etc. Here they start with 2 chunks, which is a bit different.\n\nLet's adopt that interpretation. Implementation:\n\n- current_list = items[:]\n- required_set = set(required_failure_items)\n- attempts = 0\n- granularity = 2  # number of chunks initially\n- while True:\n   - If granularity > len(current_list): break? Actually if granularity is number of chunks, that means chunk size = ceil(len/ granularity). If granularity > len(current_list), then chunk size becomes 1? But we can still have granularity > len, meaning each chunk is 1 and some chunks are empty? Usually we cap granularity to len. So we stop when we have reached the finest granularity (each item its own chunk). That is \"singleton granularity\" meaning each chunk is a single item. So we stop when granularity >= len(current_list) (or when it becomes len(current_list)). Actually when granularity = len(current_list), each chunk is exactly one item. That is singleton. So stopping condition: if granularity >= len(current_list), we can try but after that we stop. Or we can include the singleton case as the last try.\n\nAlgorithm steps:\n   - Determine chunk size: chunk_size = ceil(len(current_list) / granularity). But we need to divide the list into exactly 'granularity' chunks. Possibly non-uniform sizes. Typically we make the first (granularity - remainder) chunks of size floor(len/granularity) and last remainder chunks of size ceil(len/granularity). But simpler: we can generate start indices using step = len/granularity as float? Not deterministic. Alternatively, we could generate all possible contiguous chunks of size chunk_size? But the description says \"Try chunks from left to right\" - that suggests we consider each possible contiguous chunk of a given size (either sliding or fixed partition). Giv